{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791572516,"nonce":"Mm7V+tKC2g/X2G4h6BSYR8UNwzs=","title":"Deferred signal writes apply in reverse order, so the earlier write wins","message":"Writes made through the Signal API while a closure effect or a host effect scope is open are queued and applied afterwards. The queue was a Vec drained with pop(), so it ran last-in-first-out.\n\nReproducer: inside an effect (or between begin_host_effect and end_host_effect), call sig.set(1); sig.set(2). The signal ends at 1. Any code that writes a signal more than once in one run, such as a host effect body that sets a status and then a result, leaves the first value.\n\nCause: DEFERRED_WRITES in blinc_core::reactive was Vec\u003cBox\u003cdyn FnOnce()\u003e\u003e with pop() in drain_deferred_writes. It also queues deferred disposals, which now run in the order they were requested.\n\nFix: a VecDeque drained with pop_front(). Tests: two_writes_in_a_scope_leave_the_last and two_writes_in_a_closure_effect_leave_the_last fail with the queue drained from the back.\n\nNot changed: update() inside a scope still reads the value as of the start of the scope for each call, so two update() calls count once. That is the documented snapshot rule.","files":null}]}